WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.330
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.850
Today, I'm learning about
the Eighth Amendment

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.470
to the US Constitution,

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.870
which prohibits the government

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.650
from imposing excessive fines and bail

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.170
or inflicting cruel and unusual punishment

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.900
on individuals accused
or convicted of a crime.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.840
But what counts as excessive
or cruel and unusual?

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.280
To learn more, I sought out the help

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.270
of two experts on the Eighth Amendment.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.650
John Stinneford is the assistant director

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.440
of The Criminal Justice Center

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.640
at the University of Florida Law School.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.500
John Bessler is an
associate professor of law

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.700
at the University of Baltimore Law School.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.960
So, Professor Bessler,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.510
why were the Framers

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.130
so keen to include the Eighth Amendment?

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:44.240
Why did they wanna protect
these rights in particular?

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.670
- [Bessler] Well, these
rights were actually enshrined

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.650
in the English Bill of Rights of 1689.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.540
And so when the Americans

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.320
got in the dispute with Great Britain,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.920
they decided they wanted
to have the same rights

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.620
that Englishmen had.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:57.960
And so it was not too surprising

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.360
that when George Mason actually wrote

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.550
the Virginia Declaration
of Rights in 1776,

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:04.940
that he looked to English law

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.460
to see what rights the English had

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.020
because he wanted exactly the same rights.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.913
And so these 16 words
in the Eighth Amendment

00:01:11.913 --> 00:01:16.250
have been subject to a lot of
controversy over the years.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.970
But I think one of the reasons

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.690
that the Founders wanted this was that,

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.630
like the English would have problems

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:27.630
with the Monarch imposing
excessive bail, excessive fines,

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.820
inflicting cruel and unusual punishments,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.500
the Founders also knew that was a risk

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.010
that there was abuse from the government

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:34.843
in the United States

00:01:34.843 --> 00:01:36.930
and so they also wanted those rights.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.310
Because originally in the Constitution,

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.550
these rights were not protected against.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:45.520
And the Bill of Rights
was ratified in 1791

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.180
which ensured that there'd be protections

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.223
against these cruel
and unusual punishments

00:01:49.223 --> 00:01:51.640
and excessive bail and excessive fines.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.890
- [Stinneford] If you look
at the Eighth Amendment,

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:55.400
there's three clauses.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.500
The excessive bail clause,

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.620
the excessive fines clause,

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.350
and the cruel and unusual
punishments clause.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.400
And these all have one thing in common

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.680
which is that these are
all penalties essentially

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.940
that the government inflicts on people

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.860
usually as the result of
either being accused of crime

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.690
or of being convicted of crime.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.720
So if you've been arrested,
you're waiting for trial,

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.300
very often your only way to
get out of jail before trial

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.060
is to make bail,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.130
and of course after you've been convicted

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.610
the court might impose a fine on you

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.320
or some other kind of punishment.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.250
So the Eighth Amendment is designed

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.530
to prevent the government

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.220
from doing things that are excessive.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.520
When the government punishes a person,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.940
that's the most coercive
thing the government does

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.700
short of war.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.380
Other than shooting you in battle,

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.190
picking you up and
throwing you in a jail cell

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.440
is about as bad as it gets.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.550
And so the Framers wanted to make sure

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.660
that we had a constitutional protection

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.760
when it comes to criminal punishment.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.850
- [Kim] So, in a lot of
cases in the Bill of Rights,

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:03.130
you see the Framers reacting
to some historical evil

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:04.180
that they hoped to prevent.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.210
For example, the Third Amendment says

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:10.150
you can't quarter soldiers
in private citizens homes

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.200
because that had been such
an important tipping point

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.750
in the American Revolution.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.260
Was there something that
the Framers had in mind

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.440
as a particular historical evil
that they wanted to prevent?

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.122
- [Bessler] Well, there
was some historical evils.

00:03:25.122 --> 00:03:29.100
The English Bill of Rights
went into place in 1689,

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.730
and when that went into place

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.600
it was actually a controversy in England

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.570
around a person by the
name of Titus Oates.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:40.210
Titus Oates was somebody who
had made false accusations,

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.880
Oates had committed perjury,

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.590
resulted actually that allegation

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.890
in the execution of 15 Catholics.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.260
And the plot that he had alleged

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.730
was one to assassinate
the King of England.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.900
- [Stinneford] The question is
what to do with Oates, right?

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.900
Because as a moral matter,
he's about as bad as it gets.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.670
In fact, in 2005, English
historians voted him

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.590
the worst Briton of the 17th century

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:09.460
and the third worst Briton
of the last thousand years

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:10.293
or something like that.

00:04:10.293 --> 00:04:11.610
So a very bad guy.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.350
You can think of him as
sort of a serial killer.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:18.080
But the problem is that the
actual crime he committed

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.210
was the crime of perjury.

00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:21.840
And even though his perjury

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.440
resulted in the deaths
of many innocent people,

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.460
nonetheless he could only
be convicted of perjury

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.330
which at the time was a misdemeanor,

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.620
which meant that he could not
be executed for his crime.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.800
So when it came time for his sentencing,

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.490
the judge, Chief Justice Jeffreys,

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.140
who was a famous hanging
judge from English history,

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.377
says to Oates,

00:04:41.377 --> 00:04:43.607
"Well, Oates, we can't take your life,

00:04:43.607 --> 00:04:45.377
"we can't take your limb,

00:04:45.377 --> 00:04:48.337
"but we have something
special prepared for you."

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.040
And it turns out that what
they had prepared for him was,

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:53.410
number one, a huge fine.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.060
They fined him like 2,000 marks.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.410
They sentenced that he be
dragged across the city of London

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:00.500
while being flogged.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.710
He was dragged from Aldgate to
Newgate while being flogged.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:04.900
And then two days later,

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.060
just as the scabs were
starting to form on his wounds,

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.230
he's dragged back across
the city of London

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.180
from Newgate to Tyburn
again while being flogged.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.260
Many people think the hope was

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:15.410
that he would die from the flogging

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:18.600
but, like a cockroach in a
nuclear war, he survived.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.050
- [Bessler] This punishment

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:21.800
was actually a very severe punishment.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.853
And after the English Bill
of Rights was promulgated,

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.070
the Titus Oates punishment was challenged.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.640
And some of the members
of the House of Lords

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.890
actually called the punishment barbarous,

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:35.650
inhuman and unchristian

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:38.270
and contrary to the
English Bill of Rights.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.920
So there was no precedent to warrant

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.180
the punishments of whipping

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.910
and committing to prison for life

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.310
for the crime of perjury.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.080
This punishment was eventually remitted

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.390
in the sense that Oates
was later released.

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:49.480
- [Stinneford] Although the House of Lords

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:52.050
refused to vote to suspend
the judgment against Oates

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.740
'cause they hated his so much.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.830
They said so ill a man

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.070
shouldn't get the benefit of any relief.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:05:59.903
But they all agreed

00:05:59.903 --> 00:06:01.100
that the punishment was cruel and unusual.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.270
And what's interesting is

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.270
in the debate they say things like,

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:09.290
this punishment is contrary
to law and ancient practice.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:10.890
It is without precedent

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:13.640
and it'll be a bad
precedent for the future.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.140
So, in other words, it's cruel,

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.810
and the way we know it's cruel

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.920
is because it's so much harsher

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.510
than has previously been inflicted

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.790
for the crime of perjury.

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.040
So the Oates case shows us

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:30.240
that when the words cruel
and unusual were first used,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.980
they were used to describe punishments

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.070
that are harsher than the
common law would permit,

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:39.133
or harsher than longstanding
prior practice would permit.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.130
And this means, among other things,

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:44.520
that the cruel and
unusual punishments clause

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.880
is not limited to gruesome punishments

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.793
like torture and the rack
and all that kind of thing.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.590
'Cause in fact the punishments
inflicted on Oates,

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:56.260
although they were very harsh
for the crime of perjury,

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:58.420
were not as harsh as
some other punishments

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.360
that the common law permitted
for other crimes like treason.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:02.300
- [Kim] Wow.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.860
So that tells us a lot
about the English context

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.820
of cruel and unusual punishment.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:10.030
Do we know what cruel and unusual meant

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.693
to the Framers of the US Bill of Rights?

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:17.250
- [Stinneford] Punishments
were cruel and unusual, again,

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:18.760
if they're too harsh

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:20.850
in light of longstanding prior practice

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:23.590
for the crime for which they're inflicted.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.580
There's another problem with statutory law

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.620
or with decisions of a judge or a king,

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.880
or a president for that matter,

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.130
that's also really relevant

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.470
when we think about the
Eighth Amendment and that is

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:41.470
sometimes the government
gets really mad at someone.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:44.470
They either think of a person
as an enemy of the state

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:46.490
and they wanna inflict the
worst punishment they can

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.000
on that person,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.890
or perhaps there's a panic about
a certain group in society.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.200
For example, in American society recently,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.160
there have been panics about drug crime

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.450
or panics about sex offenses.

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:00.770
And every time that happens

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.300
the government tries to respond
with new forms of punishment

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:08.040
that are much, much harsher
than what came before.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.610
So the insight behind
the Eighth Amendment is

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.820
that when the government wants
to inflict a new punishment,

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.810
you have to compare it against
longstanding prior practice,

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:19.890
that is you have to compare
it against the common law.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:21.060
So the common law was called

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.900
the law of custom and long usage.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.920
So if something comported
with the common law,

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:28.240
it was usual.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.410
If it was contrary to the common law,

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.010
it was unusual.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.260
And that's where we get the phrase

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.780
cruel and unusual punishments.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.270
It's basically punishments that are cruel

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:41.320
in light of or in comparison to

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:42.980
longstanding prior practice.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:45.360
So the basic point of the Eighth Amendment

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.060
from a historical point of view

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:49.160
is to prohibit the government

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:52.550
from innovating in a cruel manner,

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:55.250
making up new cruel punishments

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:59.670
in response to some actual
or perceived provocation

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.200
by a criminal.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.160
- [Bessler] When the US
Bill of Rights was adopted

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.330
many years later, many decades later,

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:10.200
they had their own issues that
they were struggling with.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:15.200
And so the history shows
that the American Founders

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.300
probably meant something
different than the English meant

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:19.210
because it was done
over a 100 years later,

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:22.430
but no one knows exactly what was meant

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:24.270
when they adopted that wording.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:25.390
To give an example,

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:27.970
when the Bill was debated in Congress,

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:29.770
a representative from South Carolina

00:09:30.955 --> 00:09:32.730
said that he objected to the words

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.480
nor cruel and unusual punishments

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.540
because he said the import
of them was too indefinite.

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:40.230
And there was another legislator,

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.570
Mr. Livermore from New Hampshire,

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.297
who said that the clause seemed to express

00:09:44.297 --> 00:09:46.147
"A great deal of humanity,

00:09:46.147 --> 00:09:48.567
"in which account I
have no objection to it.

00:09:48.567 --> 00:09:50.617
"But as it seems to have no meaning in it,

00:09:50.617 --> 00:09:52.647
"I do not think it necessary.

00:09:52.647 --> 00:09:54.850
"What is meant by the
terms excessive bail?"

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:55.683
He asked.

00:09:55.683 --> 00:09:56.570
"Who are to be the judges?"

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:57.937
He also asked.

00:09:57.937 --> 00:10:00.240
"What is understood by excessive fines?"

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.390
It lies to the court to determine.

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:04.540
And so that's really where we
are today in a lot of ways.

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:05.820
The court is still deciding,

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:08.090
in this case the US Supreme
Court is still deciding

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.900
what the Eighth Amendment
language actually means today.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:12.280
- [Stinneford] The modern case law,

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:14.240
specially starting in the 1970s,

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.410
revolved a lot around the death penalty.

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:17.900
And so the question was,

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:20.260
is it still okay to execute people

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:23.600
for various crimes short of murder?

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:24.450
Since the 70s,

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:27.870
the Court has continued to
do that in a number of areas.

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:28.703
So it's said

00:10:28.703 --> 00:10:31.580
you can't execute the
mentally disabled anymore,

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:34.370
you can't execute minors,

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:37.740
you can't execute anyone
for a non-homicide offense.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.120
Although it's limited the death penalty

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:42.190
in the name of current
standards of decency,

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:45.980
it's really not clear how
the Court has set about

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:48.210
to determine whether a punishment

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:50.110
meets current standards of decency.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.750
When the Court is kind of on its own

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:54.650
saying that a punishment

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:56.800
violates current standards of decency,

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:57.750
despite the fact

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.740
that most democratically
elected legislators

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:02.373
actually approve the practice,

00:11:02.373 --> 00:11:05.890
then it looks like the Court
is acting as a political body.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.500
It's led the court to ignore

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:11.640
what I think is the
real danger of cruelty,

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.960
which is that when there's a public panic

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:16.720
and the legislature responds
by rationing up punishment

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:20.410
to new and unprecedented
levels of punishment.

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.510
And that's actually happened quite a lot

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:24.240
in the last 40 years.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.023
Every time there's a panic,

00:11:26.023 --> 00:11:28.120
you predictably see the legislatures

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.920
coming up with new punishments

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:33.590
that are much, much harsher
than what came before.

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:36.000
And so for example with sex offenders,

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.290
there are now a bunch of states

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.880
that actually impose chemical castration

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:42.560
as a form of punishment for sex offenders.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:45.020
Now, castration as a form of punishment

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:48.020
fell out of usage in the 13th century.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:50.520
We're literally getting
medieval on sex offenders.

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.210
But the Court can't do
anything really to stop it,

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:55.960
or at least hasn't,

00:11:55.960 --> 00:12:00.710
because these are very
popular forms of punishment.

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:02.100
Everyone hates sex offenders.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:04.520
- [Bessler] The UN has actually decided

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.280
that anything more than 15-days
use of solitary confinement

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.160
should not be permitted.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:11.570
Justice Anthony Kennedy

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:14.210
actually raised the issue
of solitary confinement

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:15.610
in recent opinion.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:17.280
He authored for the Supreme Court

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.060
and he actually raised
the issue on his own,

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:21.010
an oral argument at one point,

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:22.360
talking about how long

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.650
people actually spend
in solitary confinement

00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:26.340
in American prisons,

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:29.200
including on death row.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:32.510
You have cases actually
where people are spending

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:35.840
not just years but sometimes
decades on death row

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:37.720
in these kinds of conditions.

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:41.040
Justice Breyer just
wrote a dissent in a case

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:43.140
where the person had been on death row

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:44.890
for more than 40 years,

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:46.810
so literally four decades

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:50.220
in these kinds of very harsh
conditions of confinement.

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:53.100
Other countries have decided

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:56.410
that that is not something
that they want to permit

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:58.150
and they've actually set a rule

00:12:58.150 --> 00:12:59.560
that anybody that's on death row

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:01.370
for a certain number of years for example

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:03.800
would have their sentence
commuted to a life sentence

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:05.630
because of the psychological aspect

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:10.110
of waiting for one's own death.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:11.100
- [Kim] In the Bill of Rights,

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:14.620
this is the last of four
amendments, actually,

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:18.740
that are concerned with
protections for the accused.

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:22.080
So, why do you think there is
so much in the Bill of Rights

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.130
about the justice system?

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:27.000
Were the Framers particularly interested

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.760
in making sure that
the accused had rights?

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:30.837
- [Stinneford] Yeah, they were.

00:13:30.837 --> 00:13:34.180
And in particular
Americans were very devoted

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:38.210
to the idea of the common
law as a source of rights.

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:39.620
In fact, that's why we had

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:42.080
the American Revolution
in the first place,

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:46.030
was that England was denying
to Americans common law rights

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:48.480
like the right not to be taxed

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.500
without representation in Parliament

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:53.100
but also more specifically
to the criminal law

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.980
they were denying them
the right to a jury trial

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.460
in criminal cases.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.780
And so Americans wanted to make sure

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:03.020
that when the US Constitution was adopted

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:04.670
that those common law rights

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:06.880
that had built up over time in England

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:12.750
would be preserved in the new
American constitutional order.

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:16.320
And many of those rights
had to do with criminal law,

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.440
both criminal procedures

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:20.630
and to some degree
subsequence of criminal law

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:22.280
and of course criminal punishments.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.010
And, again, the reason gets back

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:26.020
to what I said at the beginning

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:28.590
which is that when the
government punishes someone,

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:31.040
that's about the worse thing it can do.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:35.470
And because the early Americans
who framed the Constitution

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:38.360
were very powerfully
concerned with liberty,

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:39.193
they wanted to make sure

00:14:39.193 --> 00:14:42.170
the government would
preserve their liberty,

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:43.810
protect their liberty,

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:46.280
and not become tyrannical.

00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:48.740
And so one of the main ways

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:50.950
that they wanted to
make sure this happened

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:53.580
was by limiting the
power of the government

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:55.660
to punish whoever it wanted to

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:57.370
for any reason that it wanted to.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:15:00.900
And so we have really the
majority of the protections

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:02.090
in the Bill of Rights

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:05.440
have to do with the protections
for criminal defendants.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.600
- [Kim] What about excessive
bail and excessive fines?

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:13.600
How can we define what
kind of financial penalty

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:17.720
is proportionate to a crime?

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.290
- [Bessler] The courts
have said, essentially,

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:21.070
they've looked at dictionary definitions,

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:24.550
that excessive means
more than it's necessary.

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:25.800
One of the core principles

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:29.660
actually go back to look at
Beccaria's work in the 1760s.

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:32.537
He talked about this
idea of a scale of crimes

00:15:32.537 --> 00:15:34.330
and a scale of punishments,

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.470
and he said that there
should be proportionality

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:37.913
between the two.

00:15:37.913 --> 00:15:39.470
And so that proportionality principle

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:43.290
is one that we're really
still wrestling with today.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:44.760
- [Stinneford] Now, the point of bail

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:47.520
is not to punish someone

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:51.840
but rather just to make sure
that they will appear at trial.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:55.130
And so the amount of money
you have to impose for bail

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:57.590
doesn't depend so much on
what crime you committed

00:15:57.590 --> 00:16:00.790
but what your financial resources are.

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:02.200
It depends partially on the crime too

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:04.200
but largely on your financial resources.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:06.190
So the amount of money necessary

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:09.490
to make sure that a poor
man appears at trial

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:10.840
is probably gonna be much lower

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:12.100
than the amount of money necessary

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.740
to make sure that a rich
man appears at trial.

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:16.850
And so it's a standard

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:18.500
that depends partly on
the nature of the crime

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:22.510
but also partially on the
nature of the offender.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:24.230
- [Kim] I imagine that what seemed

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:28.880
like cruel and unusual
punishment in the 18th century

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:32.910
might not be what we consider
cruel and unusual today.

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:37.690
For example, we don't do
whipping as a punishment anymore.

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:41.660
How has what counts as
cruel and unusual punishment

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.130
changed over time?

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:45.530
- [Bessler] The law really
changes gradually over time,

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:46.453
as you know.

00:16:46.453 --> 00:16:48.650
And so when you look back at history,

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:50.340
they actually had a large collection

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:53.010
of pretty gruesome punishments

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:56.470
back in the 18th century.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:58.400
We had non-lethal corporal punishments,

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:01.020
they used things like branding people.

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:04.440
They of course were
whipping slaves back then.

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:06.100
Slavery was still around.

00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:08.860
This is well before the Civil War ended

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:10.510
and the destitution of slavery.

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:12.740
And we also had things like ear cropping.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:15.440
People would get their ears cut off.

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:18.400
In the Crimes Act of 1790,

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:19.700
which was passed just the year before

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.403
the ratification of the Eighth Amendment,

00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:25.820
Congress actually
authorized public whipping,

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:27.240
lashing of people,

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:29.240
and it also authorized the pillory,

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:32.140
the same punishment that had
been used against Titus Oates.

00:17:32.140 --> 00:17:36.560
So there was these non-lethal
corporal punishments

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:37.430
and really the death penalty

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:40.960
is the last vestige of bodily punishment

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:44.720
that the Supreme Court has
read the Eighth Amendment

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:47.570
to allow the use of capital punishment.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:49.210
That issue is still a very live one

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:50.120
before the Supreme Court,

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:54.510
renouncing challenges about
lethal injection protocols.

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:58.720
We saw a challenge to a
protocol in Kentucky in 2008.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:03.400
We saw one to protocol
in Oklahoma in 2015.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:05.330
These of course are things
that the Founding Fathers

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:06.760
would never have envisioned.

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:08.020
- [Stinneford] Lethal injection

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:11.030
typically involves a three-drug cocktail.

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:13.770
There's a barbiturate which is
supposed to put you to sleep.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:16.520
There's a paralyzing agent
which paralyzes your body

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:18.300
and also stops your lungs from moving.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:19.950
And there's a heart-stopping agent.

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:21.840
Give the offender all three

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:25.300
and they're supposed to
die quickly and painlessly.

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:26.830
But the problem is,

00:18:26.830 --> 00:18:28.350
if the sedative doesn't put you

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:32.180
completely, deeply unconscious,

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:34.870
then the other two drugs

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:38.310
are likely to make you suffer
quite a bit before you die.

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:39.180
And so the question is,

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:43.890
is this cruel and unusual or
is it not cruel and unusual?

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:47.710
And to date the Supreme
Court has twice held

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:50.630
that lethal injection
is not cruel and unusual

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:52.120
and their main reason has been

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:55.240
that the state's not trying
to torture you to death

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:56.850
and so if maybe

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:58.710
you're sometimes accidentally
tortured to death

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:00.780
well, that's just too bad,

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:02.854
but it's not a cruel
and unusual punishment.

00:19:02.854 --> 00:19:04.120
- [Bessler] So the (mumbles) has approved

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.660
various methods of execution.

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:06.780
At the same time, however,

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:10.330
the Eighth Amendment has been
read to protect prisoners.

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:11.513
So in general the Eighth Amendment

00:19:11.513 --> 00:19:13.370
is sort of a protective shield

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:15.040
that prohibits prison guards

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:17.810
from gratuitously beating up inmates.

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:21.360
It requires prisons to provide

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.730
some level of health care to prisoners

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:25.440
because they cannot get it themselves,

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:27.150
they are sort of wards of the state

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:29.000
once they're put in prison.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:32.730
It requires inmates be fed and sheltered.

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:33.730
So in a lot of ways

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:36.560
the Eighth Amendment
is a protective shield

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:37.740
protecting inmates,

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:39.970
but then in the use of
the capital punishment

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:41.651
it becomes what I like to call,

00:19:41.651 --> 00:19:45.150
it's kind of a Dr. Jekyll
and Mr. Hyde jurisprudence

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:47.650
that the Eighth Amendment has right now.

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:49.570
- [Kim] So, we've learned
that the Eighth Amendment

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:52.090
seeks to limit the power of the government

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:54.070
in meeting out punishment to people

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:57.300
who have been accused
or convicted of a crime.

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:58.660
Although it's hard to tell

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:01.980
exactly what constitutes
excessive fines or bail,

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:04.390
in general it's accepted
that those punishments

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:07.240
should be proportional to
the crimes in question.

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:09.150
Today, one of the biggest debates

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:12.620
concerns whether or how
the Eighth Amendment

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:14.860
may limit the death penalty.

00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:16.510
To learn more about the Eighth Amendment,

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:18.620
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:20.320
Interactive Constitution

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:23.883
and Khan Academy's resources
on US government and politics.

